00:00.000 --> 00:04.000
Now, we have seen that the direct linear transformation algorithm

00:04.000 --> 00:10.000
minimizes the norm of Ah, where we can rewrite Ah to be equal to epsilon,

00:10.000 --> 00:14.000
and we'll call this epsilon here the residual vector.

00:14.000 --> 00:20.000
Hence, the direct linear transformation algorithm aims to minimize the residual vector.

00:20.000 --> 00:25.000
And each point correspondence here, xi and xi' here,

00:25.000 --> 00:30.000
contributes to a part of this error vector, which is a 2x1 vector,

00:30.000 --> 00:34.000
where the norm is called the algebraic error.

00:34.000 --> 00:39.000
Hence, what we are doing here, by minimizing Ah over here,

00:39.000 --> 00:44.000
we are minimizing what is known as the algebraic error,

00:44.000 --> 00:48.000
or algebraic distance, which is the total residual error

00:48.000 --> 00:54.000
caused by all the point correspondences that forms Ah here.

00:54.000 --> 01:03.000
And this algebraic distance here, let's denote it by the algebraic of x' and h of xi.

01:03.000 --> 01:10.000
This means that after transforming or transferring the point of xi from one image to the other image,

01:10.000 --> 01:13.000
we want to compute the algebraic distance between the two.

01:13.000 --> 01:19.000
And this is given by the error vector or the residual vector

01:19.000 --> 01:25.000
that is formed from the norm of Ah, which can be computed in this way over here.

01:25.000 --> 01:33.000
Hence, given a set of correspondences, the total algebraic error for the complete set

01:33.000 --> 01:37.000
is simply given by the sum of all the algebraic distances

01:37.000 --> 01:42.000
that is incurred by each one of these point correspondences

01:42.000 --> 01:47.000
that we can directly compute from the norm of Ah square here.

01:47.000 --> 01:51.000
So the disadvantage of minimizing the algebraic distance

01:51.000 --> 01:59.000
is that the quality is not meaningful at all in terms of geometry or statistically.

01:59.000 --> 02:06.000
This is because we are simply minimizing the error based on this linear algebra form,

02:06.000 --> 02:10.000
this linear form over here, and there is no geometrical meaning.

02:10.000 --> 02:22.000
This means that I'm not minimizing any distance geometrically in the image or in the 3D world physically.

02:22.000 --> 02:27.000
So nonetheless, it's a linear solution and thus unique.

02:27.000 --> 02:34.000
So this means that by minimizing the norm of Ah with respect to h,

02:34.000 --> 02:39.000
we are always guaranteed to have a unique solution here.

02:39.000 --> 02:42.000
And it's computationally inexpensive.

02:42.000 --> 02:48.000
Hence, the solution based on algebraic error are usually used for initialization

02:48.000 --> 02:54.000
for nonlinear minimization, where we will see that usually the steps here would be

02:54.000 --> 03:02.000
we will initialize the solution of h using algebraic distance or algebraic cost function.

03:02.000 --> 03:07.000
And then once we have this initial solution, the disadvantage of this is that

03:07.000 --> 03:10.000
it's not geometrically meaningful.

03:10.000 --> 03:17.000
This means that the solution of doing this from the SVD and from this minimization over here

03:17.000 --> 03:25.000
is not going to be as accurate as what we are going to see next compared to minimizing it geometrically.

03:25.000 --> 03:31.000
But it has an advantage that is computationally inexpensive and there is always a unique solution here.

03:31.000 --> 03:36.000
So we will use it as an initialization for the nonlinear minimization,

03:36.000 --> 03:44.000
which is using a geometric cost function that will lead to a better result or a refinement of the result.

03:44.000 --> 03:50.000
Thus, the nonlinear minimization will give us a finer solution

03:50.000 --> 03:57.000
or give us a finer polish to the solution of the problem that we want to solve.

03:57.000 --> 04:05.000
The geometric distance in the two image refers to the difference between the measured and the estimated image coordinates.

04:05.000 --> 04:09.000
So now let us first look at the transfer error in one image.

04:09.000 --> 04:16.000
Suppose that I have the image where it contains my observed point of xi prime here,

04:16.000 --> 04:22.000
