07:17.000 --> 07:27.000
In the second term here, we are transferring xi prime to the image of xi via H inverse and computing this as the error measure.

07:27.000 --> 07:31.000
And the first term is the transfer error in the first image.

07:31.000 --> 07:35.000
And the second term here is the transfer error in the second image.

07:35.000 --> 07:40.000
And again, the error is minimised over the estimated homography.

07:40.000 --> 07:46.000
This means that we want to do a mean of this over H,

07:46.000 --> 07:55.000
minimising the total symmetric transfer error between the two images over all the points.

07:55.000 --> 08:05.000
And there is a third way of computing the geometric distance as the cost function, which we call the reprojection error.

08:05.000 --> 08:14.000
Now we are seeking a homography that perfectly aligns the match points or transfer the two points.

08:14.000 --> 08:22.000
So this is a subsidiary point which we denote as xi hat and xi hat prime.

08:22.000 --> 08:33.000
What this means is that we are seeking for a homography that suppose that I have an observed point of xi and then another observed point of xi prime,

08:33.000 --> 08:42.000
which is the correspondence of xi, I'm seeking for a homography which is given by H over here.

08:42.000 --> 08:52.000
And this H is going to transfer another point here, which is my xi hat over here,

08:52.000 --> 08:59.000
to exactly the point where I desire, which I call xi hat over here.

08:59.000 --> 09:01.000
And this H is my perfect transfer.

09:01.000 --> 09:14.000
This means that the given H hat over here, I'll be able to transfer the corresponds of xi hat and xi hat prime here.

09:14.000 --> 09:21.000
They are going to be exact compared to xi and xi prime, which are corrupted with noise here.

09:21.000 --> 09:28.000
So we are going to compute this as our reprojection error.

09:28.000 --> 09:38.000
This means that given this Hx, I will be able to have and also my xi hat and my xi hat prime over here.

09:38.000 --> 09:45.000
I'm going to compute the error between xi hat and xi as my first error term here,

09:45.000 --> 09:52.000
and xi hat prime and xi prime here as my second error term here,

09:52.000 --> 09:58.000
subjected to that is a perfect transfer from the homography of H hat,

09:58.000 --> 10:04.000
such that it will perfectly transfer xi hat to xi hat prime.

10:04.000 --> 10:14.000
And minimizing this cost here will give us the best homography that minimizes this distance over here.

10:14.000 --> 10:23.000
This means that I want to make xi hat and xi hat prime close to xi and xi prime.

10:23.000 --> 10:33.000
So what's interesting about the reprojection error is that it's going to be much more accurate than the symmetric transfer error and the algebraic error.

10:33.000 --> 10:38.000
The reason is because we are not only optimizing over the homography,

10:38.000 --> 10:53.000
but we're also optimizing over x hat and x hat prime such that it ends up to have a perfect mapping or transfer via the two images using the homography that we computed.

10:53.000 --> 10:58.000
But the problem here is that although it leads to a much more accurate solution,

10:58.000 --> 11:07.000
it's also much more computationally expensive or complex because there are two additional terms that we need to optimize over now.

11:07.000 --> 11:21.000
And its complexity contrasts drastically with the simplicity of minimizing the algebraic error of this residual norm that we have looked at earlier of AH here.

11:21.000 --> 11:32.000
And we have an alternative option which lies in between the two errors, the algebraic errors, as well as the reprojection error,

11:32.000 --> 11:37.000
which trades off between the computation complexity and the accuracy.

11:37.000 --> 11:45.000
And this is the Samsung error and it lies between the algebraic and the geometric cost function in terms of complexity.

11:45.000 --> 11:51.000
But it gives a close approximation to the reprojection error in terms of accuracy.

