And next, I'll select the point that is consistent with the model.

30:23.000 --> 30:32.000
What this means is that I would have a point here, suppose I have a point here, and the error function and the error that I computed here is d1.

30:32.000 --> 30:39.000
And then I will have another point over here, which the error that I computed is d2.

30:39.000 --> 30:48.000
Now, suppose that I define a threshold, this threshold distance, which I call, suppose I call sigma d here,

30:48.000 --> 30:54.000
and d2 is actually lesser than sigma d here.

30:54.000 --> 31:03.000
So I will select it as an inlier, and d1 here is bigger than sigma d, the threshold that I have selected.

31:03.000 --> 31:07.000
It's going to be classified as an outlier here.

31:07.000 --> 31:16.000
From the randomly selected two points, I'm going to compute all the points that are within this threshold, as shown by the green points over here.

31:16.000 --> 31:21.000
And I'll repeat this and keep the record of all the support points.

31:21.000 --> 31:28.000
So I repeat the whole process by selecting multiple pairs of random two points,

31:28.000 --> 31:37.000
and then I'll compute, for each one of these, I'll compute the hypothesized line as well as the support points that are within the threshold.

31:37.000 --> 31:44.000
So for every hypothesis, I'm going to keep a record of the number of green points, which I call the support set over here.

31:44.000 --> 31:46.000
So the number of green points are the support set.

31:46.000 --> 31:52.000
I'm going to compute the cardinality of this support set in each one of the hypotheses.

31:52.000 --> 32:00.000
And finally, I'll select the hypotheses with the highest number of support sets or consistent points.

32:00.000 --> 32:06.000
There is an inlier here, and this will be all my inliers,

32:06.000 --> 32:17.000
which I will then make use of these inliers to compute the final least squares solution, where I'll ignore all the outliers from here.

32:17.000 --> 32:23.000
So here's the summary of the RANSAC algorithm, where the objective is to robustly fit a model.

32:23.000 --> 32:33.000
And in the case of the line fitting algorithm, that model is y i equals to m x i plus c to a data set S, which contains outliers.

32:33.000 --> 32:43.000
So I have a data set in the line fitting algorithm, that's x i, y i, where i here equals to one, two, and I have an observation here.

32:43.000 --> 32:46.000
And this is corrupted with outliers here.

32:46.000 --> 32:53.000
And now the algorithm is first, we randomly select a sample of S data points.

32:53.000 --> 33:00.000
So in the case of the line fitting algorithm, S here is two data points.

33:00.000 --> 33:20.000
This is the minimum number of points that we need to fit the line to compute m and c here from the data set S over here to substantiate the hypotheses.

33:20.000 --> 33:29.000
Then we'll determine the set of data points, which is also known as a support set, within a certain distance threshold t to the model.

33:29.000 --> 33:35.000
So in the line fitting algorithm, we have these two points, which is S from the S data over here.

33:35.000 --> 33:41.000
Then we will determine all the points that are in this particular threshold t here.

33:41.000 --> 33:47.000
So all these are classified as inliers and those that are outside are outliers over here.

33:47.000 --> 33:56.000
And then finally, after n trials, this means that we repeat this n number of times, we'll select the one, the trial with the largest consensus set.

33:56.000 --> 34:04.000
And then all the inlier set over here, all the inlier points here will be used to estimate the model.

34:04.000 --> 34:09.000
Here altogether, there are three parameters that needs to be set to for the RANSAC algorithm.

34:09.000 --> 34:20.000
The first parameter is the number of points as this, the number of points that we need to form the model or to solve the hypotheses of a model.

34:20.000 --> 34:24.000
In the case of the line, it's the two points, two random points.

34:24.000 --> 34:35.000
And the distance threshold for us to identify or classify whether the point for each hypothesis, a point is an inlier or outlier.

34:35.000 --> 34:42.000
And then finally, we have the number of samples. This means that we need to do this over n number of trials.

34:42.000 --> 34:52.000
So this is also a parameter that we need to set because we need to terminate the algorithm, the RANSAC algorithm after n number of trials.

34:52.000 --> 35:02.000
But n cannot be too small. If n is too small, then we wouldn't have enough samples here to contain the true solution or the best solution here.

35:02.000 --> 35:09.000
But n can also not be too big because if it is too big, then it will be computationally intractable.

35:09.000 --> 35:16.000
So typically, we'll choose the number of points as the minimum points needed to fit the model.

35:16.000 --> 35:20.000
So the two for the lines and four for homography, for example.

35:20.000 --> 35:27.000
And the distance threshold are usually set empirically or otherwise it can be set at the three sigma distance.

35:27.000 --> 35:36.000
And the number of points it exhaustively searches over the number of samples is usually unnecessary and infeasible.

35:36.000 --> 35:46.000
So there is a statistical theorem that is written in the original paper that describes the RANSAC algorithm.

35:46.000 --> 35:51.000
The best solution is actually given by this equation over here.

35:51.000 --> 35:59.000
So the probability that an algorithm never selects a set of s points which are inliers is given by this.

35:59.000 --> 36:07.000
So what it means here is that randomly select two points, for example, the probability that the algorithm in the n number of trials,

36:07.000 --> 36:19.000
it will never select the set of these two points such that these two select points are inliers to the optimal solution of the line,

36:19.000 --> 36:29.000
is given by one minus p here, where p is the probability that it will select at some point of time in this n trial,

36:29.000 --> 36:37.000
it will select the set of s points such that they are both or such that they are all inliers to the model.

36:37.000 --> 36:44.000
And this is equal to one minus w to the power of s and the whole thing to the power of n.

36:44.000 --> 36:53.000
We can see that inside here w here is the probability that any selected point is an inlier.

36:53.000 --> 37:02.000
That means that all of these s points that is selected, one of them is an inlier and this is given by the probability of w

37:02.000 --> 37:09.000
and to the power of s because we want the probability that all selected s points are inliers.

37:09.000 --> 37:18.000
So one minus w s here, it means that the probability that at least one of the s points is an outlier

37:18.000 --> 37:27.000
and to the power of n, this means that at least this means that all the n trials, all the n trials,

37:27.000 --> 37:32.000
I'm going to select all the s points such that at least one of them is going to be the outlier.

37:32.000 --> 37:41.000
Hence this is equivalent to the probability of the algorithm never selecting the whole set of s points to be inliers.

37:41.000 --> 37:57.000
And we can reformulate this a little bit by shifting the terms around to get n as the subject here.

37:57.000 --> 38:08.000
So n is the number of trials that is needed and this would be equal to log one minus p divided by log one minus w to the power of s here.

38:08.000 --> 38:14.000
And so this will give us the total number of the trials that is needed.

38:14.000 --> 38:26.000
And this particular table here shows the number of trials, the number of n that is needed for over different sample size of s over here,

38:26.000 --> 38:32.000
as well as the proportion of outliers epsilon one minus w over here.

38:32.000 --> 38:44.000
And for a fixed probability that at least one sample in the n trials is going to be all the s points are all going to be inliers.

38:44.000 --> 38:53.000
And we can see that this is going to increase exponentially with the number of sample size.

38:53.000 --> 39:09.000
And we can see that from two to eight over here for 50% of epsilon here proportional of outliers, it actually increases exponentially from 17 to 1177.

39:09.000 --> 39:15.000
And hence, as a result, it's better to keep the sample size as small as possible.

39:15.000 --> 39:20.000
That's why we need to use the minimal number of points for the model.

39:20.000 --> 39:24.000
And w here is the probability that any selected point is the inlier.

39:24.000 --> 39:27.000
So we can choose the worst case that 50%.

39:27.000 --> 39:35.000
This means that out of all the given set of points, 50% of them are outliers and 50% of them are inliers.

39:35.000 --> 39:40.000
And alternatively, w can also be decided adaptively.

39:40.000 --> 39:48.000
Within each trial, we choose the set of sample and then count the number of inliers after we form the hypothesis model.

39:48.000 --> 39:51.000
We count the number of inliers that is within this threshold.

39:51.000 --> 40:03.000
And then we'll set w to be the number of inliers divided by the number of points, the total number of points that we have observed over here.

40:04.000 --> 40:11.000
And then we can compute n adaptively from this w that we compute after every trial.

40:11.000 --> 40:18.000
And increment the sample count by one at the end of each one of these count.

40:18.000 --> 40:25.000
And if in the case where we have more than when the number of trials that we have,

40:25.000 --> 40:31.000
because we are keeping a counter to count the number of iterations, the number of trials that we have here.

40:31.000 --> 40:42.000
And in the case where in any one of these loops, the sample count, this particular trial, the number of trials is more than n,

40:42.000 --> 40:47.000
then we will stop this RANSAC algorithm.

40:47.000 --> 40:54.000
Here's a summary of the robust 2D homography computation with the RANSAC algorithm.

40:54.000 --> 41:03.000
So the objective is to compute the 2D homography between two images where the correspondences can be corrupted with outliers.

41:03.000 --> 41:11.000
So the interest points here will compute the key points in each image and then we'll get the putative correspondences.

41:11.000 --> 41:18.000
So we'll match the key points using some descriptors, for example, the SIFT or the SURF descriptors.

41:18.000 --> 41:24.000
And then, but this is going to consist of some outliers or a lot of outliers,

41:24.000 --> 41:30.000
then we are going to use the robust RANSAC algorithm for estimation,

41:30.000 --> 41:37.000
where we'll repeat for n samples where n is determined adaptively, as we have seen in the previous slide.

41:37.000 --> 41:45.000
We'll first select four random correspondences. This is the minimal set that's needed to compute the model or the homography.

41:45.000 --> 41:50.000
And then we'll calculate the distance for each putative correspondence

41:50.000 --> 41:58.000
and compute the number of outliers according to H by the number of correspondences, which is lesser than D.

41:58.000 --> 42:06.000
So this distance here, what it means here is that after we have randomly selected four point correspondences here,

42:06.000 --> 42:13.000
we compute H here and then for each of the other point correspondences that are in the image,

42:13.000 --> 42:18.000
other than the one that we have selected, the four points that we have selected in the trial,

42:18.000 --> 42:23.000
we're going to transfer these points using the computed H over here.

42:23.000 --> 42:30.000
We're going to compute the error, the geometric error, using either the Samsung error or the symmetric error.

42:30.000 --> 42:36.000
And then this error here, the total distance here, is going to determine whether,

42:36.000 --> 42:42.000
we're going to determine whether this is an inlier or outlier by comparing it with the threshold here.

42:42.000 --> 42:49.000
And finally, after n number of trials, we're going to choose the H with the largest number of inliers

42:49.000 --> 42:56.000
and make use of all the inliers to re-estimate the H, the homography again.

42:56.000 --> 43:04.000
And this will give us the optimal result or estimation of the homography that is free from outliers.

43:04.000 --> 43:09.000
So in summary, today we have looked at two cases to show the existence of homography.

43:09.000 --> 43:15.000
And we also look at how to explain the difference between algebraic, geometric and sensor errors,

43:15.000 --> 43:19.000
and then we apply them on homography estimation.

